 \paragraph{Labels.} For each lidar return within 1--80\,m, the absolute relative error (AbsRel) of the fitted prediction at that pixel is placed in its grid cell; the nearest point wins a cell. The target is $\mathbf{1}[\mathrm{AbsRel} > \tau]$ with $\tau = 0.10$, and a mask marks the labeled cells. About 9\% of cells on the $264 \times 148$ grid carry a label, about 3,600 per image, which corresponds to 0.25\% of pixels, in line with the proposal's ``under one percent''.
 
-\paragraph{Head and loss.} Three $3 \times 3$ convolutions with ReLU and widths 64, 64, 32, then a $1 \times 1$ convolution to one logit per cell. The loss is binary cross-entropy over labeled cells only, with positives weighted by the negative-to-positive ratio.
+\paragraph{Head and loss.} The head is three $3 \times 3$ convolutions with ReLU and widths 64, 64, 32, then a $1 \times 1$ convolution to one logit per cell. The loss is binary cross-entropy (BCE) over labeled cells only, with positives weighted by the negative-to-positive ratio.
 
-\paragraph{Training.} AdamW, learning rate $10^{-3}$, weight decay $10^{-4}$, cosine schedule, batch size 8, 8 epochs. The dev split is 10\% of the training scenes, capped at 3,000 images. The checkpoint kept is the one with the lowest dev loss (deviation D14). Channel groups are switched off with one flag per group, which is how the ablation runs below were made.
+\paragraph{Training.} We train with AdamW at a learning rate of $10^{-3}$ and a weight decay of $10^{-4}$, on a cosine schedule, with batch size 8, for 8 epochs. The dev split is 10\% of the training scenes, capped at 3,000 images. The checkpoint kept is the one with the lowest dev loss (deviation D14). Channel groups are switched off with one flag per group, which is how the ablation runs below were made.
 
